# iptables — real-world examples

## Watch rule hit counters with auto refresh

```sh
watch --interval 0 'iptables -nvL | grep -v "0     0"'

# Highlight changes since the last refresh
watch -d -n 2 iptables -nvL
```

## Block a port and hide it from nmap

```sh
# REJECT with icmp-port-unreachable makes the port look closed, not filtered
iptables -A INPUT -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 902 -j REJECT --reject-with icmp-port-unreachable

# --reject-with accepts:
#   icmp-net-unreachable
#   icmp-host-unreachable
#   icmp-port-unreachable   <- hide a port from nmap
#   icmp-proto-unreachable
#   icmp-net-prohibited
#   icmp-host-prohibited
#   icmp-admin-prohibited
#   tcp-reset
```

## Add a comment to a rule

```sh
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 8080 -j ACCEPT -m comment --comment "This rule is here for this reason"
```

## Remove or insert a rule by line number

```sh
# 1) Show rules with line numbers
iptables -L INPUT --line-numbers
# or: iptables -nL --line-numbers

# 2a) DELETE rule 2 from INPUT
iptables -D INPUT 2

# 2b) or INSERT at a given position
iptables -I INPUT 3 -i eth1 -p tcp --dport 21 -s 123.123.123.123 -j ACCEPT \
  -m comment --comment "FTP from the office IP only"
```

## Homelab: allow Plex on mediaserver from the LAN only

```sh
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 32400 -s 192.168.1.0/24 -j ACCEPT \
  -m comment --comment "Plex from LAN"
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 32400 -j DROP
```

## Homelab: rate-limit new SSH connections on homelab

```sh
iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -m conntrack --ctstate NEW \
  -m limit --limit 4/min -j ACCEPT
```

## Homelab: Tumbleweed note — firewalld is the default frontend

```sh
# Tumbleweed ships firewalld; check before hand-editing iptables rules
sudo systemctl status firewalld
sudo firewall-cmd --list-all
```

| Flag | What it does                            |
| ---- | --------------------------------------- |
| -A   | append rule to a chain                  |
| -I   | insert rule at a position               |
| -D   | delete rule                             |
| -L   | list rules                              |
| -n   | numeric output (no DNS lookups)         |
| -v   | verbose — show packet/byte counters     |
| -j   | jump target (ACCEPT, DROP, REJECT, ...) |
